			<div class="content" id="post-content-1622969">
									

<p>It would depend where the doctor was from exactly. He would be much more likely to call him a regional nickname, or at the very least &quot;northerner&quot; or &quot;southerner&quot;.</p>
<p>Perhaps he could say &quot;at least you're not another bleedin' septic.&quot; or &quot;nice to see someone from the old green and pleasant&quot;.</p>
<p>For what it's worth, cockney rhyming slang is, at it's root, a kind of thief-cant and is intended to obfuscate your meaning from outsiders. You wouldn't generally use the whole phrase, and particularly not the part that rhymes with your intended meaning, eg: &quot;get up them apples&quot; where the full phrase is apples and pears (stairs).</p>

							</div>

								
					
